    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d361-a1">

                <pb facs="0470" n="374" sameAs="frus:frus1899/pg374"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Terres to Mr. Powell.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Consulate-General of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Port au Prince, Haiti</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1899-03-30"><hi rendition="#italic">March 30,
                                1899</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 309.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Minister</hi>: On the afternoon of the 29th,
                    when I arrived at my residence, I found at the entrance and also on the premises
                    some fifteen or twenty Haitian soldiers, who, however, withdrew before I had
                    time to reach the gate. I inquired of one of my domestics what was their
                    mission. He replied that they had come with an order to arrest two men I had
                    employed on the premises.</p>
                <p>The following morning, in coming from my bath, I found two Haitian generals
                    stationed on the gallery of my house. I asked them what was their mission. They
                    informed me that they had an order to arrest two Spaniards that I had in my
                    employment. I demanded who had given them such orders. They replied that it was
                    an order from the minister of the interior. I told them that I did not admit of
                    any right on his part to invade my premises with an armed force under any
                    pretext whatever, and that if he desired any information from me on any matter,
                    to write to me officially and I would answer him.</p>
                <p>Mr. Minister, I most solemnly protest against any such action on the part of the
                    Haitian Government, and I lay these facts before you hoping that you will take
                    such steps as you may deem necessary to prevent a like occurrence.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John B. Terres.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
